  "type": "singleCorrect",
  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "The ratio of angular momentum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math>&nbsp;to the atomic dipole moment <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> for hydrogen like atoms and ions is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "QofnV",
      "text": "always constant and is equal to the ratio of mass to the charge of electron",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Ostmo",
      "text": "always constant and is equal to twice the ratio of mass to the charge of electron to the principal quantum number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math>",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "uorFY",
      "text": "proportional to the principal quantum number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math>",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "BiaLP",
      "text": "proportional to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math>",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The electron moving with a speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math> in the circular Bohr orbit of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math> constitutes a current of magnitude.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/bitsat/a7b5a0a6-b400-4706-8f72-baaf9b8de79c-image.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 232px;\" /></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math> is the orbital period of the electron.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>v</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>By definition of atomic dipole moment <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></math>&nbsp;its magnitude is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#183;</mo><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo></math> Angular momentum, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>r</mi></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo></math>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>L</mi><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>e</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mtext>&#160;constant&#160;</mtext></math></p>",
